Furuncles and carbuncles
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Painful firm inflammatory nodule, Central suppuration/pointing, Fluctuant mass

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-020
Reason for Visit: Extremely painful, rapidly enlarging mass on the posterior thigh causing inability to sit or walk comfortably.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented an intensely painful, expanding nodule on the thigh with new-onset systemic malaise and low-grade fever over the last 12 hours. Urgent assessment was necessary to evaluate for an aggregate carbuncle with deep tissue involvement, perform bedside source control if fluctuant, and initiate systemic antibiotics to prevent widespread cellulitis or sepsis prior to morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "There is a huge, hard lump on the back of my thigh that throbs constantly. I can't sit down at all, and I feel hot and sick."
History of Present Illness: The patient noticed a small, tender red bump on the posterior right thigh 5 days ago. Over the past 48 hours, there has been rapid enlargement into an extremely painful, firm, erythematous inflammatory nodule. Yesterday, the mass became highly fluctuant and tender, indicating mature abscess formation. Today, the patient notes central suppuration presenting as a distinct yellow-white pustular tip. The pain is severe, constant, and exacerbated by any movement. The patient reports a new onset of low-grade fever and generalized systemic malaise. They have a past history of recurrent staphylococcal infections.
Functional Status: Ambulatory, but severely limited due to mechanical pain from the abscess.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; increasing systemic symptoms.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/82 | HR: 98 | Temp: 37.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, standing during the interview, unwilling to sit due to severe localized pain.
Physical Exam: On the posterior right thigh, there is a prominent 4x4 cm firm, erythematous inflammatory nodule. The surrounding skin is hot and erythematous, extending 2-3 cm beyond the indurated edge. The center of the lesion demonstrates pronounced, ballotable fluctuance with a distinct, 3 mm yellow-white pointing tip. It is exquisitely tender to palpation. There are no multiple connecting sinus tracts (ruling out a carbuncle), but the solitary furuncle is large. Tender regional lymphadenopathy is palpable in the right inguinal crease.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current antibiotics. Previous history of MRSA swab 2 years ago.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Large mature Furuncle (Staphylococcal Abscess) with surrounding cellulitis - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Infected Epidermoid Cyst - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of a long-standing, painless, slowly growing subcutaneous nodule prior to the acute inflammatory event; presentation is classic for a primary hair follicle infection.

Carbuncle - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a single locus of fluctuation and pointing, rather than a larger, indurated inflammatory mass with multiple distinct draining sinus tracts.

Hidradenitis Suppurativa - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Solitary acute lesion on the thigh, lack of chronic double-comedones, tunneling, or recurrent scarring in typical intertriginous areas (axillae, groin).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with a large, intensely painful, mature furuncle complicated by local cellulitis and early systemic symptoms. Triage accurately flagged the rapid expansion, exquisite pain, and fever as requiring urgent assessment to prevent clinical deterioration. Given the extreme pain, visible fluctuance, and central pointing, the lesion required immediate mechanical source control. Performing a bedside incision and drainage safely released the localized pressure and purulence, providing instantaneous pain relief. Coupled with the initiation of systemic antibiotics covering potential MRSA, this out-of-hours intervention definitively aborted a progressing systemic infection and prevented an inevitable emergency department presentation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Following informed consent and local anesthesia (Lignocaine 1% with adrenaline), a simple bedside incision and drainage was performed, yielding 10ml of thick, purulent material. The cavity was flushed and a sterile dressing applied. Prescribed oral Trimethoprim/Sulfamethoxazole (Bactrim DS) 1 tablet BD for 7 days (empirically covering MRSA given patient history).
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking your antibiotics exactly every 12 hours with a large glass of water, you will maintain a steady defense in your bloodstream, ensuring the remaining bacteria are completely wiped out and preventing a recurrence. By strictly washing your hands with soap before and after touching the dressing, you will physically block the bacteria from spreading to other parts of your body or to other people.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: GP review in 48 hours for wound reassessment, packing removal (if placed), and monitoring of the cellulitis.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the surrounding redness rapidly expands down your leg, if you develop a high fever over 39.0C, confusion, or if the extreme throbbing pain returns despite the drainage, present immediately to the nearest Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Abscesses must heal from the inside out. Premature closure of the skin can lead to recurrence.
Clinical Course: The sharp, throbbing pressure should be dramatically improved immediately following the drainage. The surrounding redness and mild fever should resolve over 48 hours on antibiotics.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor the red skin around the dressing. Seek emergency medical care if the redness spreads rapidly or if you begin vomiting and cannot tolerate the antibiotics.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The lump was a sealed pocket of pus caused by a deep hair follicle infection. Antibiotics alone cannot penetrate the pus, so opening it was necessary to drain the infection. The antibiotics will now clear the remaining inflammation in the surrounding skin.
Actionable Steps: Keep the dressing clean and completely dry. Do not attempt to squeeze or press on the wound.

